"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,365\nWhat sleep does\n\n4\n00:00:12,165 --> 00:00:15,903\nRecord from cells in a rat's hippocampus while it explores.\n\n5\n00:00:16,273 --> 00:00:20,699\nCells that fired together in the same places fire together again later,\n\n6\n00:00:20,904 --> 00:00:22,627\nwhile the rat is in deep sleep.\n\n7\n00:00:23,152 --> 00:00:26,178\nThe pattern was weaker in sleep before the exploring.\n\n8\n00:00:26,623 --> 00:00:28,783\nThe brain seems to replay the day.\n\n9\n00:00:28,953 --> 00:00:32,102\nDeep, slow wave sleep is where this happens most.\n\n10\n00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,440\nLarge slow waves sweep across the cortex.\n\n11\n00:00:35,721 --> 00:00:38,544\nShort bursts called spindles ride on them.\n\n12\n00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:42,252\nIn the hippocampus, brief ripples carry the replay.\n\n13\n00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:44,181\nTheir timing lines up,\n\n14\n00:00:44,407 --> 00:00:48,730\nwhich is thought to help move new memories from the hippocampus toward the cortex.\n\n15\n00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:50,346\nCan the replay be steered?\n\n16\n00:00:50,705 --> 00:00:54,663\nPeople learned the locations of card pairs while smelling roses.\n\n17\n00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:56,560\nDuring deep sleep that night,\n\n18\n00:00:56,623 --> 00:00:58,180\nthe scent was released again.\n\n19\n00:00:58,714 --> 00:01:02,309\nThe next day, they remembered ninety seven percent of the locations,\n\n20\n00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:05,126\nagainst eighty six percent without the cue.\n\n21\n00:01:05,595 --> 00:01:07,603\nThe same scent during dream sleep,\n\n22\n00:01:07,623 --> 00:01:09,813\nor while awake, did not help.\n\n23\n00:01:09,983 --> 00:01:12,821\nThis is called targeted memory reactivation.\n\n24\n00:01:13,362 --> 00:01:16,534\nA meta-analysis of ninety one experiments,\n\n25\n00:01:16,554 --> 00:01:17,904\nwith about two thousand people,\n\n26\n00:01:18,216 --> 00:01:20,484\nfound a real but small effect,\n\n27\n00:01:20,759 --> 00:01:23,172\nunder a third of a standard deviation,\n\n28\n00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,159\nand only in non REM sleep.\n\n29\n00:01:26,329 --> 00:01:28,508\nSleep also matters before learning.\n\n30\n00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:30,808\nAfter one night without sleep,\n\n31\n00:01:31,149 --> 00:01:34,878\npeople encoded new material with less hippocampal activity,\n\n32\n00:01:35,229 --> 00:01:39,486\nand later retained about nineteen percent less than people who had slept.\n\n33\n00:01:39,656 --> 00:01:40,451\nA caution.\n\n34\n00:01:40,854 --> 00:01:42,629\nMost of this comes from labs,\n\n35\n00:01:42,715 --> 00:01:47,499\nwith simple materials, and cueing memories during sleep is not a classroom tool.\n\n36\n00:01:48,032 --> 00:01:52,284\nThe reliable message is simpler: sleep after learning helps it stick,\n\n37\n00:01:52,612 --> 00:01:55,806\nand sleep loss before learning makes it harder to get in.\n\n38\n00:01:55,976 --> 00:01:56,763\nFor teaching.\n\n39\n00:01:57,209 --> 00:02:00,495\nAvoid deadlines that reward all nighters,\n\n40\n00:02:00,602 --> 00:02:03,266\nlike midnight submissions before a morning exam.\n\n41\n00:02:03,806 --> 00:02:06,427\nTeach students that sleep is part of studying,\n\n42\n00:02:06,612 --> 00:02:08,104\nnot time taken from it.\n\n43\n00:02:08,531 --> 00:02:11,960\nAnd review hard material the day after it is introduced,\n\n44\n00:02:12,202 --> 00:02:14,482\nonce sleep has had its first pass.\n\n45\n00:02:14,652 --> 00:02:16,438\nLearning does not stop at night.\n\n46\n00:02:16,794 --> 00:02:19,273\nSleep is when the brain files the day.\n\n"
